;;; *forbid* misses a refutation whose activator chain first appears at a
;;; node — M1e2 **W17**, register row `W17/forbid-chain-1`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1e2.2.17 (2026-11-04) from
;;; S1e2.2.16's parked note, where it was
;;; `build/s1e2.2.16/extra/node-chain-sym.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two candidates, `(p a)` and `(p2 a)`. While `a` has no `p2`, `mk`
;;; derives `(q a)` from `(p a)`. `(q a)` is an activator: the plan of `q`
;;; it compiles asserts `(r2 a)`, another activator, and the plan of `r2`
;;; that one compiles concludes `(false)`. Root holds neither candidate, so
;;; it derives none of this and compiles no plan of `q` or of `r2`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `{(p a)}` dies, its `(false)` resting through two activators on
;;; `(absent (p2 a))`, and `{(p a), (p2 a)}` lives: a dead hole. `p2` is a
;;; relation a commitment can extend, so this is *forbid* with F2's *via,
;;; per activator* (the user's rulings of 2026-11-02 and 2026-11-04): the
;;; program is refused at every command that takes the universe, as
;;; `forbid-chain-0.ein`, the same chain one link shorter, is.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.17 it loaded: R three states, ∅, {(p2 a)} and
;;; {(p a), (p2 a)}, the one dead hole {(p a)}, exit 0, in every mode and
;;; `ein oracle` (`ein solve FILE` at `3efd3f5f`). *forbid* read a rule with
;;; parameters root has no plan of from its pattern only when its activator
;;; relation was in D, and D's closure follows reads, not activators: `q`'s
;;; is (root's plan of `mk` writes it), `r2`'s is not (only `q`'s plan, which
;;; root never compiled, writes it), so `r2` was never read, and the
;;; read-out's status line named no site: *exercised, by* and an empty list.
;;; Since then *forbid* reads such a rule whenever its activator relation is
;;; in D⁺, D followed through the rules root has no plan of, and every
;;; question about a commitment exits 1: `rule 'r2': concludes (false) from
;;; its activator, which rule 'q' derives resting on (absent …) over p2`.
;;; Studied past the refusal, the status names `q`, the activator site the
;;; chain runs through. `forbid-chain-2.ein` is the chain one link longer.

(relation seed T :why "{?1} is a seed") (relation p T :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation p2 T :why "p2 holds of {?1}") (relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}") (relation r2 T :why "r2 holds of {?1}")

(seed a)

(hrule g  () :match (seed ?x) :assert (p ?x) :why "guess that p holds of the seed {?x}")
(hrule g2 () :match (seed ?x) :assert (p2 ?x) :why "guess that p2 holds of the seed {?x}")

(rule mk () :match (and (p ?x) (absent (p2 ?x))) :assert (q ?x) :why "p holds of {?x} and p2 does not, so q holds of {?x}, switching rule q on for it")
(rule q  (?n) :match (seed ?n) :assert (r2 ?n) :why "q holds of the seed {?n}, so r2 holds of it, switching rule r2 on for {?n}")
(rule r2 (?k) :match (seed ?k) :assert (false) :why "r2 holds of the seed {?k}, which cannot be")
